<b>Opening Reception</b><br/>Monday, October 19, 2026 6:00 PM– 8:00 PM<br/>Summit Pavilion<b>Opening Plenary Session</b><br/>Tuesday, October 20, 2026 9:00 AM– 10:30 AM<br/>Reno Ballroom<b>President's Opening Remarks</b><br/><i>Part of Opening Plenary Session</i><br/><br/>Tuesday, October 20, 2026 9:00 AM– 9:10 AM<br/>Reno Ballroom<br/>Welcome and opening remarks from HFES President, Caroline Cao<b>Navigating the Frontier Between AI and Human Factors: A Three-Decade Journey in Text Input</b><br/><i>Part of Opening Plenary Session</i><br/><br/>Tuesday, October 20, 2026 9:10 AM– 10:00 AM<br/>Reno Ballroom<br/>The smartphone keyboard is one of the most used interfaces in the world, and arguably a forerunner of human-centered AI in the mass market. It is also a clear case study of a central challenge facing our field: balancing the rapid evolution of computing and AI machinery with a deep understanding of human performance, behavior, and habit. This talk uses three decades of research, design, and engineering in mobile text input to examine that dynamic. It traces a line from foundational models of human performance and &quot;laws of action&quot; such as tapping, steering, and gesturing, through the invention of gesture typing and keyboard layout optimization, to the large-scale probabilistic error correction and next word prediction serving over a billion people daily in Gboard, and most recently to AI-augmented voice writing. As the AI frontier accelerates, accurately understanding the human side becomes more consequential than ever&#8212;both for text input and for the broader design of human-AI interaction.<b>CEDM1: Trust and Transparency in AI-Assisted Decision Making</b><br/>Tuesday, October 20, 2026 11:00 AM– 12:00 PM<br/>Carson 3<b>Do you feel like using GenAI? Scenario emotional valence impact on potential generative artificial intelligence use</b><br/><i>Part of CEDM1: Trust and Transparency in AI-Assisted Decision Making</i><br/><br/>Tuesday, October 20, 2026 11:00 AM– 11:20 AM<br/>Carson 3<br/>Generative artificial intelligence (genAI) is becoming increasingly ubiquitous, creating questions about factors that are influencing its use. This study explored whether the presence of emotions in decision-making scenarios impacted the possible use of genAI writing tools. We presented 35 scenarios that required a written product, with some associated with the 6 basic human emotions (sadness, anger, enjoyment, fear, surprise, disgust) and the others created as neutral emotional conditions. College student participants were asked whether they would use a genAI tool to complete the task, and then to identify the emotion present. Most participants were not fixed in the decision to use AI tools or not, and did vary their preferences across scenarios. There were significant effects of emotion on the potential use of genAI assistance, with a higher intended adoption for fear and surprise emotional contexts. Emotion strength also impacted potential AI use, with reported AI trust and task complexity increasing in stronger-rated scenarios. However, the overall rate of AI use was generally low, and participants noted hesitance for using genAI tools in daily tasks. Implications for automation use and trust theories are discussed.<b>HART1: Calibrating Trust and Reliance</b><br/>Tuesday, October 20, 2026 11:00 AM– 12:00 PM<br/>Crystal 1-2<b>SD1: Human Factors in System Design Across Domains</b><br/>Tuesday, October 20, 2026 11:00 AM– 12:00 PM<br/>Nevada 2-3<b>What is the Best Word to Describe AI Uncertainty: Confidence, Certainty, Reliability, or Accuracy?</b><br/><i>Part of CEDM1: Trust and Transparency in AI-Assisted Decision Making</i><br/><br/>Tuesday, October 20, 2026 11:20 AM– 11:40 AM<br/>Carson 3<br/>AI systems are becoming increasingly common in decision-making tasks. However, these systems often generate probabilistic responses, resulting in uncertainty in their outputs. Understanding this uncertainty can help users make robust decisions, such as when to trust the agent and whether to seek more information. Interpreting uncertainty is not always straightforward, though; past work shows there is variability in how people interpret probabilistic information (e.g., weather forecasts). This study builds on that research by analyzing how people interpret probabilistic AI responses. In this survey-based experiment (N = 632), participants interpreted AI uncertainty statements. The word used to describe the uncertainty (confidence, certainty, reliability, or accuracy) varied between trials. Participants often treated confidence and certainty as indicators of how sure the agent was about its output, whereas interpretations of reliability and accuracy were more variable. These findings can guide how AI systems communicate uncertainty, supporting more accurate user interpretations and robust decision-making.<b>RISE: Pragmatic Evaluation of Human-AI Reliance Calibration</b><br/><i>Part of HART1: Calibrating Trust and Reliance</i><br/><br/>Tuesday, October 20, 2026 11:40 AM– 12:00 PM<br/>Crystal 1-2<br/>This presentation will introduce the Reliance in Intelligent Systems Evaluation (RISE) framework, a practical structure for assessing how people use AI advice in human-AI teaming contexts. It will discuss why technical performance alone is insufficient for evaluating AI-enabled systems, particularly in safety-critical work, and why reliance calibration is an important human factors concern. The presentation will outline how RISE organises reliance measures into key categories, including raw AI use, appropriate and inappropriate reliance, verification and monitoring behaviour, trust, confidence, workload, and task outcomes. It will also describe a proposed minimum reporting standard for human-AI reliance analysis, intended to help researchers and practitioners compare designs, identify misuse or disuse risks, and interpret whether AI support is being used safely and effectively.<b>The All-Too-Human Challenge of Mental Measurement: On the Limits of Large Language Models’ Self-Reflection for Objective Benchmarking</b><br/><i>Part of SD1: Human Factors in System Design Across Domains</i><br/><br/>Tuesday, October 20, 2026 11:40 AM– 12:00 PM<br/>Nevada 2-3<br/>As large language models (LLMs) are deployed in new applications, specifically safety-critical domains like nuclear power, scrutable evaluation methods for those LLMs must be used. This paper introduces the Generative Artificial Intelligence Nuclear Evaluation (GAINE) framework for applying verification, validation, and benchmarking across the LLM lifecyle of design, development, deployment, and monitoring. For each of these evaluation types and phases, there are system and human methods. The human-in-the-loop evaluations of LLMs are essential to overcome the limits of self-evaluation of LLMs and to ensure the appropriateness and completeness of the evaluations.<b>Can AI Effectively Screen Complex Literature: A Case Study of Driver Engagement Research</b><br/><i>Part of ST1: AI-Enabled Methods and Technologies for Transportation</i><br/><br/>Tuesday, October 20, 2026 1:30 PM– 1:50 PM<br/>Carson 2<br/>AI-assisted tools are increasingly used to support literature screening, yet their effectiveness in complex, interdisciplinary domains remains unclear. This study evaluates the performance of an AI-assisted systematic review tool (Elicit) during abstract screening for a human factors research question on driver engagement in Level 2 automation. Elicit retrieved 500 records and applied screening criteria to generate inclusion decisions and ratings. We manually reviewed 335 records to assess the AI tool&#8217;s precision, discriminative power, and uncertainty. Results showed low overall precision (10.71%). However, among the papers ultimately included through complete manual review, 94% had received the AI tool&#8217;s highest rating (4.9), suggesting that the rating may help prioritize records for subsequent human screening even though overall precision is limited. Analysis of screening criteria revealed that methodological criteria such as human participants and empirical evidence, were easily satisfied but weak at filtering irrelevant studies, whereas topic-related criteria such as automation level and engagement effectiveness exhibited stronger discriminative potential but greater uncertainty. These findings suggest that AI still struggles with conceptually complex distinctions. We conclude that AI-assisted screening can improve efficiency but still requires human expertise to resolve conceptual ambiguity, particularly in interdisciplinary domains such as human factors.<b>ST1: AI-Enabled Methods and Technologies for Transportation</b><br/>Tuesday, October 20, 2026 1:30 PM– 2:30 PM<br/>Carson 2<b>Large-Language-Model-Agent-Enabled Analysis of Semi-Structured Interviews: Comparing Driver Experience Across Partial Automation Systems</b><br/><i>Part of ST1: AI-Enabled Methods and Technologies for Transportation</i><br/><br/>Tuesday, October 20, 2026 1:50 PM– 2:10 PM<br/>Carson 2<br/>SAE Level 2 (L2) partial automation changes the driving task, shifting the driver's role from active operator to supervisor, raising questions about trust, satisfaction, perceived limitations, and system understanding. This study presents a locally hosted, auditable large language model (LLM) agent pipeline for analyzing 161 semi-structured post-study interviews from a 30-day naturalistic driving study of Tesla Autopilot, Cadillac Super Cruise, and Volvo Pilot Assist. The pipeline segmented transcripts into semantic units and coded each unit by question theme, answer theme, subtheme, and sentiment, enabling structured comparison across systems while preserving traceability to source evidence. Results showed significant vehicle-level differences in sentiment, with satisfaction-related responses strongly positive but performance-limitation narratives strongly negative. Validation against manually coded interview data showed high accuracy for answer subthemes and sentiment. Findings suggest that driver satisfaction with partial automation can coexist with uncertainty about system limits and automation boundaries.<b>Effects of SA-Based Augmented Reality HUDs on Driver Situation Awareness in Level 3 ADS Under Adversarial Attack</b><br/><i>Part of ST1: AI-Enabled Methods and Technologies for Transportation</i><br/><br/>Tuesday, October 20, 2026 2:10 PM– 2:30 PM<br/>Carson 2<br/>An adversarial attack on Level 3 automated driving systems (ADS) introduces maliciously crafted perturbations to perception inputs, potentially leading to unsafe system decisions. To mitigate the safety risks posed by adversarial attacks and improve drivers&#8217; situation awareness (SA) during takeover requests (ToR), this study proposes the implementation of SA-based augmented reality head-up displays (AR-HUDs). Forty participants took part in a laboratory experiment, completing a total of eight driving trials in a simulator under different SA-based AR-HUD conditions during adversarial attacks: baseline, SA1, SA2, SA3, SA1+SA2, SA1+SA3, SA2+SA3, and SA1+SA2+SA3. Driver SA was measured using the Situation Awareness Global Assessment Technique (SAGAT), and data were analyzed using linear mixed-effects models. The present study revealed a significant effect of AR-HUD conditions on drivers&#8217; overall SA during L3 ADS takeovers under a road-surface induced adversarial attack. These findings provide experimental evidence that embedding SA level-specific cues within a multimodal ToR can enhance SA recovery under adversarial conditions. Furthermore, the results offer design implications for future multimodal ToR systems, highlighting the importance of incorporating SA level-specific cues to support more effective SA recovery.<b>Operationalizing Value: Measuring Real-World Impact in High-Consequence Systems</b><br/><i>Part of Tuesday Mid-Day Lightning Lessons</i><br/><br/>Tuesday, October 20, 2026 2:35 PM– 2:45 PM<br/>Lightning Lesson Theater, Nevada Foyer<br/>Across high-consequence industries, there is a growing emphasis on demonstrating that technologies deliver meaningful value to the people who use them. In healthcare, recent work has sought to define digital health product value based on the perspectives of those who develop, evaluate and use them (Goldsack et al., 2025). Similarly, recent Department of War (DoW) reforms emphasize a more product-and warfighter-centric delivery model focused on delivering value to operators (US Department of War (DoW), 2025). This shift raises a shared human factors challenge: how do we define and measure value in high-consequence systems? Relying on commercial telemetry, such as feature utilization, creates dangerous blind spots. If programs define value simply by how many operators use a tool daily, they risk deprecating low-usage features that serve as mission-critical functions for specialized subgroups. In high-consequence contexts, high usage does not inherently equal high value, and generalized metrics often mask critical capability gaps. This talk presents a mixed-methods value assessment framework for defining and measuring the operational impact of software in high-consequence domains. We explore multidimensional metrics that evaluate system performance across broad and specialized user populations. By integrating quantitative analytics with qualitative insights, this framework prevents flawed design decisions and ensures a holistic understanding of value. Furthermore, we demonstrate how continuous value assessment supports system validation. By measuring value across diverse user populations, practitioners provide the empirical evidence to track human-system maturity alongside technological readiness. This data-driven approach ensures that agile, iterative development cycles deliver user-validated capabilities that truly enhance mission effectiveness.<b>Tuesday Mid-Day Lightning Lessons</b><br/>Tuesday, October 20, 2026 2:35 PM– 2:55 PM<br/>Lightning Lesson Theater, Nevada Room<b>Methodological Considerations and Practical Insights for Vigilance Research in Nontraditional Settings</b><br/><i>Part of Tuesday Mid-Day Lightning Lessons</i><br/><br/>Tuesday, October 20, 2026 2:45 PM– 2:55 PM<br/>Lightning Lesson Theater, Nevada Foyer<br/>The study of vigilance has a long history dating back to World War II when Norman Mackworth initiated the first empirical research of the phenomenon in response to a real-world issue. Since Mackworth&#8217;s pioneering research, most experiments have been conducted in the laboratory, using simple static displays and artificial tasks. Conducting vigilance research in nontraditional settings outside the laboratory can introduce distinct methodological challenges in the planning and implementation phases. This presentation examines these methodological obstacles and provides practical guidance that can transform methodological challenges into opportunities for discovery and insight. The presentation will include examples of vigilance research conducted in the context of operator simulators for human-automation systems and industrial inspection to illustrate methodological challenges during planning and implementation. Such challenges include recruiting participants, allowing extra time to build trust and rapport with participants or accommodate simulator familiarization, balancing the realism of vigilance stimuli with the need for sufficient data, adapting data collection tools for participants who may have little or no post-secondary school education, creating representative objects encountered in the real world to serve as neutral signals during simulated driving, and designing realistic countermeasures appropriate for field settings. Overcoming these challenges provides valuable observations and insights that advance the study of vigilance and inform the design of countermeasures to mitigate the vigilance decrement. Practical guidance offered during this presentation can facilitate future vigilance research in simulators and in the field.<b>Identifying Informative Physiological Indicators and Time Scales for Inferring Human Cognition During Automated Driving: An Information-Theoretic Approach</b><br/><i>Part of ST2: Driver Attention and Human State Monitoring</i><br/><br/>Tuesday, October 20, 2026 3:00 PM– 3:20 PM<br/>Carson 2<br/>We present an information-theoretic framework for identifying which physiological indicators, and at what temporal scales, are most informative of changes in human cognitive states (trust, workload, risk perception) during conditionally automated (SAE Level 3) driving. We analyze data collected from an in-person driving simulator study in which participants interact with a conditionally automated vehicle in a single continuous drive. Evaluating physiological signals, including heart rate and galvanic skin response, through mutual information enables assessment of informational relevance without assuming linear relationships or predefined model structures. This approach is then suitable for continuous, non-event-based settings. The results suggest that informative physiological responses emerge over intermediate time scales (50-70 seconds) and that no single indicator is universally optimal across individuals. These findings highlight the importance of using prediction models that are personalized to the user for effective cognitive state estimation.<b>ST2: Driver Attention and Human State Monitoring</b><br/>Tuesday, October 20, 2026 3:00 PM– 4:00 PM<br/>Carson 2<b>UX1: User Experience and Automation</b><br/>Tuesday, October 20, 2026 3:00 PM– 4:00 PM<br/>Nevada 2-3<b>Interpretable Modeling of Driver Attention Shifts with Vision-Language Model</b><br/><i>Part of ST2: Driver Attention and Human State Monitoring</i><br/><br/>Tuesday, October 20, 2026 3:20 PM– 3:40 PM<br/>Carson 2<br/>Driver attention is central to safe driving, but most current AI systems represent attention only as heatmaps, which show where a driver looked without explaining why attention shifted. This limits their usefulness for understanding driver situation awareness and for designing transparent driver-assistance systems. This project introduces VISTA, a vision-language modeling framework that converts driver attention shifts into structured, human-readable explanations. Instead of only predicting gaze locations, VISTA describes the driving scene, the driver&#8217;s current focus, the likely next focus, and the reason for the attention shift. Using the Berkeley DeepDrive-Attention dataset, we identify important attention transitions by measuring changes between consecutive gaze maps. These selected moments are paired with image frames and gaze overlays, then converted into four-sentence explanations using GPT-4o with human refinement. A LLaVA-NeXT vision-language model is adapted with efficient fine-tuning to learn this explanation format under limited supervision. Results show that the proposed few-shot model substantially improves over zero-shot and one-shot baselines across lexical, semantic, entity-alignment, and human-evaluation metrics. By grounding driver attention in scene entities and rationales, VISTA offers a more interpretable way to model attention shifts and may support safer, more transparent human-AI collaboration in automated driving.<b>Connecting AV Behavior to Future Adoption: An On-Road Robotaxi Study</b><br/><i>Part of UX1: User Experience and Automation</i><br/><br/>Tuesday, October 20, 2026 3:40 PM– 4:00 PM<br/>Nevada 2-3<br/>It is too often assumed in technology development that observing and measuring human interaction with that technology is not possible until after a prototype is created. We show here a counter-example, of measuring human responses to simulated vehicle behavior in a real autonomous vehicle in public traffic. We used Behavioral Intention to extrapolate from a specific in-car experience to the likelihood of future repeat ridership, based on its a validated link to future behavior. This method allows a lightweight measure, collectable in nearly any circumstances, to compare the adoption or revenue impact of multiple experiences before those experiences have been implemented. In this case, we discovered substantially more patience with in-traffic AV stops than we anticipated, with Behavioral Intention not decreasing substantially until stop duration was over 90 seconds. We used a private validation data set and internal assumptions about ride value and frequency to create financial projections comparing these different stop duration experiences to support decision-making around work prioritization and resources as well as vehicle behavior criteria. We suggest that Behavioral Intention and its sibling measure, Behavioral Expectation, can be used in robotics and automation more broadly to understand future usage behavior and guide system development.<b>Break</b><br/>Tuesday, October 20, 2026 4:00 PM– 4:30 PM<b>Exploration of the role of contextual factors in reducing decision fatigue (DF) in nurses</b><br/><i>Part of Tuesday Afternoon Lightning Lessons</i><br/><br/>Tuesday, October 20, 2026 4:05 PM– 4:15 PM<br/>Lightning Lesson Theater, Nevada Foyer<br/>Decision fatigue (DF) is a complex, transient state characterized by cognitive, emotional, and motivational changes that can occur following repeated or prolonged decision-making. The concept of DF recognizes that individuals have finite cognitive and self-regulatory resources, such that sustained decision-making demands may affect subsequent decision quality, medical errors, self-control, motivation, and willingness or ability to engage effectively in additional decisions. Industrial engineering (IE) provides a systems-based approach to understand how people, processes, technology, and work environments interact to influence performance. From an IE perspective, decision fatigue can therefore be examined not solely as an individual problem, but as an outcome that is affected by the broader healthcare system. Recent research suggests that DF is influenced by individual and contextual risk and protective factors, including cognitive, emotional, behavioral, socio-demographic, ethical, and environmental influences. Such risk factors may include work overload, fatigue, level of professional experience and expertise and educational preparation (e.g., length of training). Alternatively, protective factors may include healthcare technologies including decision aids, electronic health records, clinical decision-support systems, alerts and notifications, information presentation, and other technology-related systems. This presentation explores a model of how contextual factors may influence decision fatigue among nurses. Identification of significant risk or protective factors that affect adverse outcomes can provide opportunities to redesign workflows, policies, staffing practices, and other aspects of the work environment to better support nurses&#8217; decision-making and reduce adverse health outcomes.<b>Tuesday Afternoon Lightning Lessons</b><br/>Tuesday, October 20, 2026 4:05 PM– 4:25 PM<br/>Lightning Lesson Theater, Nevada Room<b>Assessing Efficacy & Usability of Exoskeletons in Reducing Fatigue & Musculoskeletal Discomfort in Construction Workers</b><br/><i>Part of Tuesday Afternoon Lightning Lessons</i><br/><br/>Tuesday, October 20, 2026 4:15 PM– 4:25 PM<br/>Lightning Lesson Theater, Nevada Foyer<br/>Background Work-related musculoskeletal disorders (WMSDs) are among the most prevalent occupational health concerns in construction, with the low back and shoulders most commonly affected. Controlling WMSD risk exposures is particularly challenging given the physically demanding, variable, fast-paced, and dynamic nature of construction work. These injuries contribute to accelerated worker retirement, compounding an aging workforce and immigration policy pressures to drive a critical labor shortage in the industry. Exoskeletons offer a promising intervention, reducing physical demands by decreasing muscular activity and joint loading, which in turn is expected to lower WMSD risk. However, most exoskeleton efficacy studies have been laboratory-based, leaving a clear gap in evaluating effectiveness in the field. Methods This ongoing, mixed-methods field study assesses the usability, effectiveness, and barriers to the adoption of arm- and back-support exoskeletons among construction workers in Northern California. Participants were fitted with a task-appropriate exoskeleton, trained on its use, and wore it for up to 4 weeks. Surveys were administered after a 1-hour work trial, at the end of every week of use, and upon study exit. This presentation will discuss results from up to 10 construction workers. Results Preliminary results suggest reduced fatigue and high usability (mobility, wearability, comfort). Barriers related to cost and a fear of dependency were consistent across users.  Discussion Findings will inform adoption pathways for exoskeletons in construction. This work supports efforts to improve worker retention and expand the labor pool, particularly among women, older workers, and individuals managing injury or high fatigue.<b>PD1/HPM3: Never Enough Time: Tasks and Automation</b><br/>Tuesday, October 20, 2026 4:30 PM– 5:30 PM<br/>Nevada 2-3<b>An Agent-Based Model of GenAI in Creative Writing: Novelty Gains and Diversity Declines</b><br/><i>Part of PD1/HPM3: Never Enough Time: Tasks and Automation</i><br/><br/>Tuesday, October 20, 2026 5:10 PM– 5:30 PM<br/>Nevada 2-3<br/>Generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) is being rapidly adopted in education. Its effects on creativity unfold at both individual and collective levels. We develop an agent-based model that represents students' creative outputs in a two-dimensional idea space to examine how GenAI assistance in creative writing shapes individual creativity and classroom-level idea diversity. Simulations show that GenAI increases individual creativity, with disproportionately larger gains for less creative students, while reducing collective diversity through idea homogenization. Critically, the model reveals a more nuanced, non-linear pattern of collective diversity than empirical accounts of homogenization alone: collective diversity can increase at low-to-moderate adoption rates before reaching a tipping point and declining at high adoption rates. These findings highlight the need to evaluate GenAI in education using both individual performance metrics and collective system outcomes.<b>Surface Transportation TG Bus. Mtg.</b><br/>Tuesday, October 20, 2026 5:30 PM– 6:30 PM<br/>Nevada 2-3<b>Student Reception</b><br/>Tuesday, October 20, 2026 6:30 PM– 7:30 PM<br/>Tahoe<b>BOHSI Panel on Emerging Research Topics in Human Systems Integration</b><br/>Wednesday, October 21, 2026 9:00 AM– 10:30 AM<br/>Nevada 1<br/><p>The Board on Human-Systems Integration (BOHSI) of the National Academies of Sciences, Engineering, and Medicine convenes experts to advance understanding of how people and organizational systems interact with technology. In this panel, BOHSI will share perspectives on emerging research needs in human systems integration across healthcare, where new technologies are reshaping clinical work and patient safety; workplace safety, where automation and AI are changing worker performance and well-being; and aviation, where evolving automation is redefining the roles of pilots, controllers, and maintainers. The panel will also summarize findings from a BOHSI supported study on Emerging Trends in Aviation Safety. The session will close with an open discussion inviting attendees to help shape the research agenda and identify opportunities for the human factors and ergonomics community to contribute.</p><b>CEDM4: Old Problems, New AI: Human Factors as an AI Safety Enabler</b><br/>Wednesday, October 21, 2026 9:00 AM– 10:30 AM<br/>Carson 3<br/><p>AI has rapidly become a pervasive component of complex sociotechnical systems across domains such as healthcare, transportation, and retail. While the pace of AI adoption has been driven by innovation pressure and a &#8220;move fast, break things&#8221; mentality, these approaches are misaligned for safety critical contexts where system failure can result in severe consequences. Ensuring safety in these applications requires deliberate design of the AI from the perspective of a joint cognitive system. A common narrative frames AI as so novel that its risks cannot yet be understood, leading to rapid deployment and siloed safety research. Much of the AI safety literature remains internally focused, with limited integration of established human factors (HF) knowledge. However, practitioners are not starting from scratch. Many challenges associated with AI have been extensively studied within HF and systems engineering. This panel aims to apply HF principles of system performance, safety, and interaction to AI implementation. Panelists will explore how existing knowledge can guide safer integration and discuss strategies for communicating these insights to non-HF stakeholders. By reframing AI as an extension of known system challenges, this discussion highlights the critical role HF can play in shaping effective, resilient human&#8211;AI systems moving forward.</p><b>HART6: Bridging Human–AI Interaction and Human–Robot Interaction: Shared Challenges, Converging Futures</b><br/>Wednesday, October 21, 2026 9:00 AM– 10:30 AM<br/>Crystal 1-2<br/><p>As artificial intelligence becomes more common in everyday life, researchers are increasingly asking how people can work safely, effectively, and confidently with intelligent systems. This panel brings together experts in human&#8211;AI interaction and human&#8211;robot interaction to explore where these fields overlap, where they differ, and what that means for the future of human-centered system design. The discussion will focus on shared challenges such as trust, transparency, mental workload, usability, teamwork, and safety across both embodied systems (e.g., robots) and non-embodied systems (e.g., decision-support tools and conversational AI). Panelists will draw on examples from healthcare, home settings, transportation, defense, and workplace technologies to examine how intelligent systems can be designed and evaluated to better support human performance and well-being. A key goal of the panel is to bridge research communities that have often developed separately, despite facing many of the same practical and scientific questions. By connecting these perspectives, the session will highlight common design principles, identify research gaps, and encourage collaboration across disciplines. The panel will also include discussion of training, mentorship, and future workforce development, helping to shape a more integrated and human-centered approach to the next generation of intelligent systems.</p><b>Coffee Break</b><br/>Wednesday, October 21, 2026 10:30 AM– 11:00 AM<br/>Nevada Room<b>HART7: Teaming Concepts and Testbeds</b><br/>Wednesday, October 21, 2026 11:00 AM– 12:00 PM<br/>Crystal 1-2<br/><p>This study explores the impact of designing AI teammates to support teamwork processes in Human-AI Teams (HATs), comparing reactive and proactive support&#8217;s influence on teamwork experiences. HATs achieve shared objectives through taskwork and teamwork processes. Teamwork processes between humans are well documented, such as goal setting, plan development, and coordination. However, it is less clear how AI teammates can be designed to support these processes in HATs. Furthermore, more attention is needed on how initiative levels of the AI teamwork support would impact the HATs dynamics. Two levels of initiative of a Wizard of Oz AI teammate (RoboAnt) were compared in a gamified testbed HiveMind. Reactive RoboAnt responded to teamwork requests when prompted. Proactive RoboAnt initiated and engaged in teamwork processes. The findings show significantly higher counts of teamwork processes with proactive AI across preparation, execution, and evaluation phases and better team performance. This work offers insights on designing AI teammates to support teamwork processes in HATs.</p><b>Researcher-Driven Insights for Human-Agent Teaming Testbed Development</b><br/><i>Part of HART7: Teaming Concepts and Testbeds</i><br/><br/>Wednesday, October 21, 2026 11:00 AM– 11:20 AM<br/>Crystal 1-2<br/><p>Human-Agent Teaming (HAT) is becoming increasingly important as AI systems take on collaborative roles. However, HAT research depends on experimental testbeds to study human-agent teams. This research builds on prior work that cataloged existing HAT testbeds by identifying and characterizing user needs for future HAT testbeds. In this study, nine HAT researchers were interviewed about their experimental design practices and testbed usage. Affinity diagramming was used to identify themes about pain points with existing testbeds and user needs for future testbeds. Participants indicated growing interest in using AI technologies in testbeds, to design experiments or serve as realistic teammates. Participants emphasized the importance of agent-agnostic testbeds that enable integration with external agents. These findings inform requirements for more flexible HAT testbeds that address current limitations and accommodate future research directions.</p><b>A New Theory of Robot Conflict and Compromise</b><br/><i>Part of HART7: Teaming Concepts and Testbeds</i><br/><br/>Wednesday, October 21, 2026 11:20 AM– 11:40 AM<br/>Crystal 1-2<br/><p>Intelligent robots increasingly possess capabilities for making humanlike judgments and decisions in complex environments. The scope for &#8220;reasonable disagreements&#8221; between humans and artificial systems is also increasing. Conflicts can arise over both tasking, such as analyzing a problem, and teaming issues, such as dividing responsibilities. Optimizing human-robo